window.docContents[89].resolve({"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.Ratchet.LibsignalSession/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___Ratchet___LibsignalSession":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/Ratchet › LibsignalSession.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof and symbolic proof 6 headline theorems depend on it 655 of 831 lines trusted source ↗trust banner in the header: B2, trusted: definitions used by the statement; the closure computes B3 for the symbolic proofRead the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nDeclarations on this page (77)def maxForwardJumpsdef maxMessageKeysdef maxReceiverChainsabbrev ShaCompressabbrev PubKeyFnstructure Cryptodef PublicKey.serializestructure PrivateKeystructure KeyPairdef KeyPair.generatestructure IdentityKeydef IdentityKey.serializedef macIdsabbrev RootKeystructure ChainKeystructure MessageKeyGeneratorstructure MessageKeysdef MessageKeyGenerator.generateKeysdef ChainKey.nextChainKeydef ChainKey.messageKeysdef rootCreateChaininductive SpqrDirectionstructure Spqrdef versionBytedef serializeContentdef computeMacdef SignalMessage.newdef SignalMessage.verifyMacdef SignalMessage.verifyMacWithAddressesdef aesCbcEncryptdef aesCbcDecryptstructure SenderChainstructure ReceiverChainstructure RatchetStateabbrev Mutdef Mut.execdef liftEdef zoomdef findReceiverChainIndexdef findReceiverChainKeydef takeRootKeydef evictReceiverChainsdef dhRatchetStepdef takeSkippedKeydef storeSkippedKeydef setReceiverChainKeydef advanceChaindef consumeMessageKeydef ensureReceiverChainstructure TripleRatchetdef TripleRatchet.onRatchetdef TripleRatchet.decryptstructure OutgoingTripleRatchetdef OutgoingTripleRatchet.encryptstructure SessionStatedef versiondef takeRatchetStatedef takePqRatchetStatedef clearUnacknowledgedPreKeyMessagedef TripleRatchet.fromSessionStatedef TripleRatchet.applyToSessionStatedef tryDecryptWithStatedef recordErrordef tryDecryptFromRecorddef OutgoingTripleRatchet.fromSessionStatedef OutgoingTripleRatchet.applyToSessionStatedef encryptAddressesdef messageEncryptdef newdef addReceiverChaindef setSenderChaindef setUnacknowledgedPreKeyMessagedef initializeInitiatorSessiondef initializeRecipientSessionstructure SignalHeaderdef SignalMessage.headerdef signalWireCodec\n\nlibsignal's double-ratchet session as a pure functional modelTrusted: definitions used by the statement. The theorems of Computational/Theorems/ use every definition in this file: read them. The theorems here are checked by Lean. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.Method-for-method pure functional transcription of a single libsignal v0.105.4 double-ratchet / triple-ratchet session (rust/protocol/src/{ratchet,double_ratchet,triple_ratchet,session_management,protocol}.rs, with wire and error types imported from LibsignalSignalMessage.lean). Inner &mut self methods run in Mut σ α := ExceptT SignalProtocolError (StateM σ) α (retaining partial mutations on error), while session-level tryDecryptFromRecord and messageEncrypt preserve the input record on error by operating on a clone (DEV-LS16); panics (overflow-checks = true, panic = \"abort\") surface as SignalProtocolError.panic (DEV-LS19).Primitives (Crypto), protobuf encoding (SignalMessageProtoCodec, fixed to protoSignalCodec by the headline theorem, DEV-LS21), and SPQR (Spqr, an untrusted per-message HKDF salt source, DEV-LS24, DEV-LS25) are parameterized; X25519 is byte-level (X25519Fn, linked to an abstract group via X25519RealizesDh, DEV-LS29). Relative to ACD19 Fig. 9, libsignal initializes from PQXDH with Bob's signed pre-key (DEV-LS22, DEV-LS26), steps the DH ratchet eagerly on receive (DEV-LS1, DEV-LS2), derives message keys via chain HMAC plus salted HKDF (DEV-LS14), identifies epochs by ratchet key rather than epoch number with clear-text headers and 8-byte truncated MACs over identity keys and raw wire bytes (DEV-LS8, DEV-LS12, DEV-LS13, DEV-LS15, DEV-LS20, DEV-LS23), and bounds skipped keys and receiver chains (DEV-LS6, DEV-LS9, DEV-LS10, DEV-LS11, DEV-LS31).DEV-LS28 (libsignal): scope of the model. Covers one non-self session (self_session = false, distinct identity keys) started from abstract initial keys: Sesame archived-session loops, PQXDH, PreKeySignalMessage wrapping, stores, identity trust checks, registration IDs, and the 30-day staleness check are out of scope here (LibsignalSessionApi.lean adds PreKeySignalMessage, stores, trust checks, and staleness for the public API). Session records use typed keys and Seed-form skipped keys, excluding malformed-protobuf InvalidSessionStructure branches.\n\n\n\nConstants (consts.rs)\n\ndef maxForwardJumpsL58–66 ↗#consts::MAX_FORWARD_JUMPS (consts.rs:8).DEV-LS10 (libsignal): bounded receiver state. ACD19 Fig. 6 and Fig. 8 keep every skipped key and every epoch. libsignal rejects a jump of more than maxForwardJumps = 25000 messages, keeps at most maxMessageKeys = 2000 skipped keys per chain and at most maxReceiverChains = 5 receiver chains. The model's Fig. 6 instance uses maxSkip = 25000; the reductions answer the other rejections themselves. Effect: constants (maxForwardJumps + 1 = 25001 lookahead slots in fsSlotCount qR = 2·qR + 25001) and restricts the correctness game (DEV-LS6).\n\ndef maxMessageKeysL68–69 ↗#consts::MAX_MESSAGE_KEYS (consts.rs:9): stored skipped keys per receiver chain.\n\ndef maxReceiverChainsL71–72 ↗#consts::MAX_RECEIVER_CHAINS (consts.rs:10).\n\nKeys (core/curve.rs, core/curve/curve25519.rs, identity_key.rs)\n\nabbrev ShaCompressL76–77 ↗#The SHA-256 compression function (abstract F).\n\nabbrev PubKeyFnL79–82 ↗#Public-key derivation on 32 secret bytes (PrivateKey::derive_public_key_bytes, core/curve/curve25519.rs:161–163: x25519_dalek::PublicKey::from(&StaticSecret), i.e. EdwardsPoint::mul_base_clamped(x).to_montgomery()).\n\nstructure CryptoL84–95 ↗#The primitives the session model is parameterized by (all abstract).\n\ndef PublicKey.serializeL97–98 ↗#PublicKey::serialize (core/curve.rs:122–132): 0x05 ‖ key (33 bytes).\n\nstructure PrivateKeyL100–103 ↗#Curve25519 private key: 32 clamped key bytes (core/curve/curve25519.rs:27–38).\n\nstructure KeyPairL105–109 ↗#KeyPair (core/curve.rs:320–324).\n\ndef KeyPair.generateL111–115 ↗#KeyPair::generate (core/curve.rs:327–341): clamp 32 LE bytes from coins and derive the public key.\n\nstructure IdentityKeyL117–120 ↗#Identity public key (IdentityKey, identity_key.rs).\n\ndef IdentityKey.serializeL122–123 ↗#The 33-byte serialization 0x05 ‖ key of an identity key (IdentityKey::serialize).\n\ndef macIdsL125–129 ↗#sender.serialize() ‖ receiver.serialize() as the wire AEAD's 66-byte SerializedIdentityKeys, the first two updates of compute_mac (protocol.rs:226–227).\n\nChain keys, message keys, root keys (ratchet/keys.rs)\n\nabbrev RootKeyL133–135 ↗#A root key (RootKey { key: [u8; 32] }, ratchet/keys.rs:186–188): its 32 LE bytes as a BitVec 256, as Ratchet.RootKey.\n\nstructure ChainKeyL137–141 ↗#ChainKey { key: [u8; 32], index: u32 } (ratchet/keys.rs:142–145).\n\nstructure MessageKeyGeneratorL143–148 ↗#MessageKeyGenerator::Seed((seed, counter)) (ratchet/keys.rs:13–17, 29–31, 54–60): 32-byte seed HMAC(chain key, 0x01) and u32 counter.\n\nstructure MessageKeysL150–156 ↗#MessageKeys { cipher_key, mac_key, iv, counter } (ratchet/keys.rs:91–97).\n\ndef MessageKeyGenerator.generateKeysL158–164 ↗#MessageKeyGenerator::generate_keys(pqr_key) on a Seed (ratchet/keys.rs:32–36) = MessageKeys::derive_keys(seed, pqr_key, counter) (:100–118): HKDF(salt = pqr_key, ikm = seed, \"WhisperMessageKeys\", 80) split 32/32/16, exactly HKDF.deriveMessageKeys.\n\ndef ChainKey.nextChainKeyL166–178 ↗#ChainKey::next_chain_key (ratchet/keys.rs:165–170): key' = HMAC(key, 0x02) (the chain step Ratchet.chainHmac), index + 1. The u32 addition panics at index = u32::MAX (overflow checks are on in libsignal's release profile, Cargo.toml:250–251).DEV-LS19 (libsignal): u32 counter, 0-based on the wire, panic at u32::MAX. ACD19's message indices are 1-based and unbounded. libsignal's counter is a u32, the wire carries i − 1, and next_chain_key panics at u32::MAX. The reductions treat an index ≥ 2³² as a structural rejection, and the theorem assumes 2(q + 1) + 25002 < 2³² (hq32). Effect: none for realistic sessions (the bound is vacuous long before, DEV-LS31).\n\ndef ChainKey.messageKeysL180–183 ↗#ChainKey::message_keys (ratchet/keys.rs:172–177): the generator with seed HMAC(key, 0x01) and the chain's index.\n\ndef rootCreateChainL185–192 ↗#RootKey::create_chain(self, their_ratchet_key, our_ratchet_key) (ratchet/keys.rs:199–218): the X25519 agreement (InvalidKeyAgreement if all zero, core/curve.rs:296–309) and the root KDF, via Ratchet.createChain; the new chain key has index 0.\n\nSPQR (abstract)\n\ninductive SpqrDirectionL196–200 ↗#spqr::Direction (A2B for the initiator, B2A for the recipient, ratchet.rs:86, 149).\n\nstructure SpqrL202–234 ↗#The SPQR interface (crate spqr 1.6.0, src/lib.rs): state PQ (upstream the serialized bytes SerializedState = Vec<u8>, :43; here abstract, so a game can plug in any state machine), pq_ratchet messages as bytes (SerializedMessage = Vec<u8>, :44, they go on the wire), coins PQC:* init dir pqrKey (spqr::initial_state, :211): the initial state from PQXDH's pqr_key; both parties use the same key (directions A2B / B2A).* send state coins (spqr::send, :264): the new state, the message key (the HKDF salt of the message keys; none = no salt) and the pq_ratchet bytes; none = Err.* recv state msg (spqr::recv, :355): the new state and the message key; .error true = Error::StateDecode (mapped to InvalidState), .error false = any other error (mapped to InvalidMessage, triple_ratchet.rs:242–251).* sendPanics state coins / recvPanics state msg: spqr::send / spqr::recv panics on these inputs. libsignal is built with panic = \"abort\", so the session operations abort (SignalProtocolError.panic) at the call site; the values of send / recv on such inputs are never used.No property is assumed. Upstream moves the state out of the session with mem::take (state/session.rs:677–679), leaving an empty Vec; the model leaves default : PQ ([Inhabited PQ]; for PQ = List (BitVec 8) it is [], exactly upstream).\n\nSignalMessage (protocol.rs:62–308)\n\ndef versionByteL238–240 ↗#The version byte ((message_version & 0xF) << 4) | CIPHERTEXT_MESSAGE_CURRENT_VERSION (protocol.rs:106).\n\ndef serializeContentL242–252 ↗#The bytes before the MAC (serialized of SignalMessage::new before extend_from_slice(&mac), protocol.rs:93–109): versionByte ‖ protobuf { ratchet_key = Some(serialize), counter = Some, previous_counter = Some, ciphertext = Some, pq_ratchet = None if empty, addresses }.\n\ndef computeMacL254–266 ↗#SignalMessage::compute_mac (protocol.rs:214–235): the first 8 bytes of HMAC-SHA256(mac_key, sender.serialize ‖ receiver.serialize ‖ message). The mac_key.len() != 32 error (:220–222) cannot occur (fixed-size key). Equals the wire AEAD's tag: bytesToWord64LE (computeMac …) = (hmacScheme F).tag macKey (macIds s r ‖ message).DEV-LS15 (libsignal): identity keys and addresses inside the MAC. ACD19 Fig. 8 authenticates only the header h as AD. libsignal MACs both identity keys (sender first) with the wire bytes, and Alice's messages carry the serialized addresses until her first successful receive, which the receiver checks. The Fig. 8 instance orders the identity keys by epoch parity (lIds). Effect: none (the theorem assumes the instance uses libsignal's identity keys, hid).\n\ndef SignalMessage.newL268–286 ↗#SignalMessage::new (protocol.rs:79–128). addresses is the already serialized serialize_addresses(sender, recipient) (:91–92, 237–252): none if no addresses are passed or they are not valid service ids.DEV-LS20 (libsignal): the wire format. ACD19 Fig. 8 sends (h, e) with e ← Enc(K, h, m). libsignal sends version ‖ protobuf{ratchet_key, counter, previous_counter, ciphertext, pq_ratchet?, addresses?} ‖ 8-byte MAC: AES-256-CBC with PKCS#7, then HMAC-SHA256 truncated to 8 bytes over the raw bytes. The receiver parses the counter before it checks the MAC. The proof models this as a per-message AEAD on the wire AEAD (lAead). Effect: constants (the 8-byte tag gives the forgery term macForgeryProb ≥ 2⁻⁶⁴, DEV-LS23).\n\ndef SignalMessage.verifyMacL288–295 ↗#SignalMessage::verify_mac (protocol.rs:160–173): split off the last 8 bytes and compare them with compute_mac of the rest (ct_eq). serialized.len() ≥ 9 holds for every message built by new / try_from.\n\ndef SignalMessage.verifyMacWithAddressesL297–304 ↗#SignalMessage::verify_mac_with_addresses (protocol.rs:175–212): the MAC, then, if the message carries addresses, they must equal expected = serialize_addresses(sender, recipient) (none = not serializable ⇒ reject). The wire AEAD's addressCheckPasses. The Result error of compute_mac cannot occur.\n\nAES-256-CBC (crypto/aes_cbc.rs)\n\ndef aesCbcEncryptL308–313 ↗#aes_256_cbc_encrypt (crypto/aes_cbc.rs:26–35) on any-length plaintext: cbcPkcs7EncryptOnly E ℓ (PKCS#7, CBC) at ℓ = |ptext|, as bytes (LE blocks). The BadKeyOrIv error cannot occur (fixed-size key and IV).\n\ndef aesCbcDecryptL315–319 ↗#aes_256_cbc_decrypt (crypto/aes_cbc.rs:37–53): cbcDecRaw (none = BadCiphertext; BadKeyOrIv cannot occur).\n\nThe ratchet state (double_ratchet.rs:24–55)\n\nstructure SenderChainL323–327 ↗#SenderChain { ratchet_key, chain_key } (double_ratchet.rs:46–50).\n\nstructure ReceiverChainL329–341 ↗#Receiver chain (session_structure::Chain, proto/storage.proto:11–33, double_ratchet.rs:304–309): peer ratchet public key, chain key, and stored skipped keys (newest first).DEV-LS9 (libsignal): per-chain skipped-key stores instead of FS-AEAD instances. libsignal keeps a sender chain and up to 5 receiver chains, each storing up to 2000 skipped keys as 32-byte seeds expanded with the receiving message's SPQR salt (bounds in DEV-LS10).\n\nstructure RatchetStateL343–351 ↗#RatchetState (double_ratchet.rs:31–42), with receiverChains ordered oldest first.\n\nEffects\n\nabbrev MutL355–357 ↗#A Rust method fn(&mut σ, …) -> Result<α, SignalProtocolError>: the state after the call is returned on success and on error (what upstream leaves in *self).\n\ndef Mut.execL359–361 ↗#Run a Mut action: (result, final state).\n\ndef liftEL363–366 ↗#Lift a pure Result (no state change).\n\ndef zoomL368–374 ↗#Run a Mut action on a field of the state (self.ratchet.m(…) from a method of the outer struct).\n\n\n\ndef findReceiverChainIndexL380–383 ↗#find_receiver_chain_index (double_ratchet.rs:370–380): the first receiver chain whose sender_ratchet_key equals their.\n\ndef findReceiverChainKeyL385–397 ↗#find_receiver_chain_key (double_ratchet.rs:257–269): the chain key of that chain; Err(missing receiver chain key) if it is None.\n\ndef takeRootKeyL399–401 ↗#take_root_key (double_ratchet.rs:174–176): replace the root key by 32 zero bytes.\n\ndef evictReceiverChainsL403–406 ↗#Keep the last maxReceiverChains chains: the while len > 5 { remove(0) } loop (double_ratchet.rs:310–312).\n\ndef dhRatchetStepL408–436 ↗#dh_ratchet_step (double_ratchet.rs:271–320), both half-steps, with coins the 32 bytes of KeyPair::generate(csprng):1. missing sender chain if none (no change);2. take the root key (now zero in self);3. receiving half-step create_chain(their, our sender private key) (may fail: InvalidKeyAgreement, root key left zero);4. fresh key pair; sending half-step create_chain(their, new private key) (may fail likewise);5. previous_counter = sender index saturating_sub 1, root key, push the receiver chain (their, receiver chain key, []), evict to 5, new sender chain. Returns the receiver chain key.\n\ndef takeSkippedKeyL438–450 ↗#take_skipped_key (double_ratchet.rs:322–344): in the chain for their (if any), find the first stored key with index = counter (position), remove it and return it; none if there is no such chain or key. (from_pb cannot fail on a Seed-form key.)\n\ndef storeSkippedKeyL452–460 ↗#store_skipped_key (double_ratchet.rs:346–361): insert(0, key) into the chain for their (panics if there is none), then pop() the oldest if more than 2000.\n\ndef setReceiverChainKeyL462–468 ↗#set_receiver_chain_key (double_ratchet.rs:363–368): set the chain key of the chain for their (panics if there is none).\n\ndef advanceChainL470–482 ↗#The loop while chain_key.index() < counter { store_skipped_key(their, chain_key.message_keys()); chain_key = chain_key.next_chain_key(); } (double_ratchet.rs:244–247) with fuel bounding the iterations (consumeMessageKey passes counter − index, which is exactly the number of iterations; with fuel = 0 the loop stops).\n\ndef consumeMessageKeyL484–502 ↗#consume_message_key (double_ratchet.rs:203–253) for the receiver chain of their with chain key chainKey and message counter:* counter < index: take_skipped_key, or DuplicatedMessage(index, counter) (no change);* counter − index > max_forward_jumps: InvalidMessage(too far into the future) (no change);* otherwise store the skipped keys index … counter − 1 (newest first, at most 2000), set the receiver chain key to the key after counter, return the key at counter.\n\ndef ensureReceiverChainL504–510 ↗#ensure_receiver_chain (double_ratchet.rs:185–195): the chain key of the chain for their if there is one, else dh_ratchet_step (which consumes coins).\n\n\n\nTriple ratchet (triple_ratchet.rs)\n\nstructure TripleRatchetL516–522 ↗#TripleRatchet (triple_ratchet.rs:160–165).\n\ndef TripleRatchet.onRatchetL524–526 ↗#Lift a RatchetState mutation onto TripleRatchet.ratchet.\n\ndef TripleRatchet.decryptL528–552 ↗#TripleRatchet::decrypt (triple_ratchet.rs:215–298): advances/consumes the receiver chain key (persisting on later failure), steps spqr.recv, verifies the MAC and address blob over raw bytes, AES-CBC decrypts, and commits newPqrState.\n\nstructure OutgoingTripleRatchetL554–563 ↗#OutgoingTripleRatchet (triple_ratchet.rs:41–49).\n\ndef OutgoingTripleRatchet.encryptL565–582 ↗#OutgoingTripleRatchet::encrypt (triple_ratchet.rs:86–135): steps spqr.send, derives salted message keys, AES-CBC encrypts and MACs via SignalMessage.new, and advances the sender chain key (panicking at u32::MAX).\n\nThe session state and the session-level operations\n\nstructure SessionStateL586–601 ↗#Ratchet-relevant fields of SessionState / SessionStructure (state/session.rs:141–171, proto/storage.proto:10–68).\n\n\n\ndef versionL605–606 ↗#SessionState::session_version (state/session.rs:178–183): 0 means 2.\n\ndef takeRatchetStateL608–615 ↗#take_ratchet_state(self_session = false) (state/session.rs:625–635) with RatchetState::from_pb (double_ratchet.rs:71–99): move the receiver chains out (left empty), copy root key, sender chain, previous counter; max_forward_jumps = MAX_FORWARD_JUMPS. (from_pb's errors are about malformed bytes, not representable here.)\n\ndef takePqRatchetStateL617–619 ↗#take_pq_ratchet_state (state/session.rs:677–679): move the SPQR state out (left empty).\n\ndef clearUnacknowledgedPreKeyMessageL621–623 ↗#clear_unacknowledged_pre_key_message (state/session.rs:566–598).\n\n\n\ndef TripleRatchet.fromSessionStateL627–636 ↗#TripleRatchet::from_session_state (triple_ratchet.rs:176–193): take the ratchet and SPQR states (mutating state), read the identity keys (missing remote identity key if absent).\n\ndef TripleRatchet.applyToSessionStateL638–645 ↗#TripleRatchet::apply_to_session_state (triple_ratchet.rs:199–202) with RatchetState::apply_to_pb (double_ratchet.rs:105–117): write root key, previous counter, sender chain, receiver chains and the SPQR state back.\n\n\n\ndef tryDecryptWithStateL649–661 ↗#try_decrypt_with_state (session_management.rs:566–606, self_session = false): build a TripleRatchet from state (which moves fields out of it), decrypt, and on success write the ratchet back and clear the unacknowledged pre-key flag. On error, state is left as from_session_state / the failed decrypt left it (the caller discards it).\n\ndef recordErrorL663–671 ↗#The error try_decrypt_from_record returns for a failed try_decrypt_with_state with a single session: DuplicatedMessage as is (session_management.rs:432); a panic is never collected (it aborts / unwinds); every other error is collected and becomes InvalidMessage(msg_type, \"decryption failed\") (:436–455 for PreKey, :527–554 for Whisper after the empty previous-session loop).\n\ndef tryDecryptFromRecordL673–692 ↗#try_decrypt_from_record (session_management.rs:368–556) for a record with a current session record and no previous sessions: on a clone, check the version (UnrecognizedMessageVersion, collected), run try_decrypt_with_state; store the clone only on success. A version mismatch is collected (InvalidMessage(msg_type, \"decryption failed\")); other errors are mapped by recordError. On error the record is unchanged.DEV-LS16 (libsignal): reject leaves the state unchanged only through the caller's clone. ACD19 Def. 7 (A) asks that a rejected ciphertext leaves the state unchanged. libsignal's inner methods do not: on error they leave a zeroed root key or a removed skipped key. try_decrypt_from_record works on a clone and stores it only on success, so Def. 7 (A) holds at this level. Effect: none.\n\n\n\ndef OutgoingTripleRatchet.fromSessionStateL696–709 ↗#OutgoingTripleRatchet::from_session_state (triple_ratchet.rs:52–79): read the sender key pair and chain key (missing sender chain), take the SPQR state, check the version fits in a u8, read the identity keys (missing remote identity key).\n\ndef OutgoingTripleRatchet.applyToSessionStateL711–718 ↗#OutgoingTripleRatchet::apply_to_session_state (triple_ratchet.rs:81–84): set_sender_chain_key (state/session.rs:417–440; keeps the sender key pair) and the SPQR state. (set_sender_chain_key with no sender chain would create one with empty keys; it is called only after from_session_state found a sender chain.)\n\n\n\ndef encryptAddressesL722–727 ↗#The addresses message_encrypt passes to encrypt: serialize_addresses(local, remote) iff the session is unacknowledged (Some(local_address) at session_management.rs:104, None at :117).\n\ndef messageEncryptL729–745 ↗#message_encrypt's ratchet part (session_management.rs:43–151): on the loaded record, OutgoingTripleRatchet::from_session_state, encrypt (with encryptAddresses), then apply_to_session_state and store. On error nothing is stored: the record is unchanged. For an unacknowledged session upstream wraps the result in a PreKeySignalMessage (out of scope: the inner SignalMessage is returned); the staleness check, identity trust and store calls are out of scope.\n\nSession initialization (ratchet.rs, state/session.rs)\n\ndef newL749–755 ↗#SessionState::new (state/session.rs:146–171) without alice_base_key and registration ids: no chains, previous_counter = 0, no pending pre-key.\n\ndef addReceiverChainL757–761 ↗#add_receiver_chain (state/session.rs:342–366): push, then remove index 0 if more than 5.\n\ndef setSenderChainL763–766 ↗#set_sender_chain (state/session.rs:373–387).\n\ndef setUnacknowledgedPreKeyMessageL768–771 ↗#set_unacknowledged_pre_key_message (state/session.rs:509–547), as called by process_prekey_bundle right after initialize_alice_session (session.rs:270–275).\n\n\n\ndef initializeInitiatorSessionL775–790 ↗#initialize_initiator_session (ratchet.rs:67–112) from abstract PQXDH outputs (root key rootKey, chain key chainKey with index 0, pqrKey): generate the first sending key pair from coins eagerly, create_chain(root key, their ratchet key = Bob's signed pre-key, new private key), the SPQR state spqr.init A2B pqrKey; the session has a receiver chain for theirRatchetKey with chainKey, and the new sender chain.\n\ndef initializeRecipientSessionL792–801 ↗#initialize_recipient_session (ratchet.rs:137–172) from abstract PQXDH outputs (root key, chain key, pqrKey; SPQR state spqr.init B2A pqrKey): the session's sender chain is our ratchet key pair (Bob's signed pre-key) with chainKey (index 0); no receiver chain. Upstream private keys are always clamped (PrivateKey::new, From<[u8; 32]>, PrivateKey::deserialize, core/curve/curve25519.rs:34, 170–175, core/curve.rs:242–248); the caller passes a clamped key pair (e.g. KeyPair.generate).\n\nBridge to the wire AEAD's WireCodec\n\nstructure SignalHeaderL805–813 ↗#Header fields of a SignalMessage (everything in the MACed bytes except the ciphertext body).\n\ndef SignalMessage.headerL815–832 ↗#Extract the SignalHeader of a SignalMessage.\n\ndef signalWireCodecL819–828 ↗#libsignal's SignalMessage as the wire AEAD's WireCodec: encode = the bytes before the MAC of SignalMessage::new; parse ser = SignalMessage::try_from (ser ‖ 8 bytes) (the MAC bytes are not read by try_from).\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/Ratchet/LibsignalSession.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.Ratchet.LibsignalSession/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___Ratchet___LibsignalSession"},"/Tier-2___-definitions/Symbolic___Proofs___/Symbolic.Proofs.SpecLemmas.ElfBindingProofDefs/#file-Symbolic___Proofs___SpecLemmas___ElfBindingProofDefs":{"contents":"Paper › Symbolic/Proofs/SpecLemmas › ElfBindingProofDefs.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the symbolic proof 1 headline theorem depend on it 4 of 37 lines trusted source ↗trust banner in the header: none; the closure computes B4 for the symbolic proofRead the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.No theorem of the theorem folders uses this file: only headline theorems stated in proof files do. The tier tables do not count it, and its trust banner refers to the theorems of the theorem folders.\n\nCLI extern-contract satisfiability predicateDefines CompExternsSatisfiableCli, asserting the joint satisfiability of ExternInterpretationTotalOnUsedSymbols, ExternContractsHoldCli, and HooksMatchInterpretation.\n\n\n\ndef CompExternsSatisfiableCli used only by headline theorems stated in proof filesL32–38 ↗#Joint satisfiability of extern totality, CLI extern contracts, and hook agreement.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"Symbolic/Proofs/SpecLemmas/ElfBindingProofDefs.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/Symbolic___Proofs___/Symbolic.Proofs.SpecLemmas.ElfBindingProofDefs/#file-Symbolic___Proofs___SpecLemmas___ElfBindingProofDefs"}});